Recommended Reading Paths
There are two useful ways to read the notebooks.
If you want the quickest sense of what the method produces, start with Notebook 06. It shows the enriched event table and the final demonstration figures.
If you want to understand the full workflow, read the notebooks in order:

clean donated watch histories;
clean donated search histories;
document metadata collection;
link watch events, metadata, subscriptions, searches, and availability signals;
estimate watch time from metadata duration and the next observed watch event;
summarize the enriched exposure measures.

How To Interpret The Pipeline
The notebooks are designed to make the data transformation visible. Each step keeps intermediate outputs so that readers can inspect what changed between raw Takeout-style files and the final enriched exposure table.
The most important conceptual shift happens between notebooks 01 and 04:

after notebook 01, the project has cleaned watch events;
after notebook 04, those watch events have become enriched exposure events.

That enrichment does not reveal exactly why YouTube showed a video or why a participant watched it. Instead, it creates transparent indicators that can be used to study aggregate exposure patterns.
For example:

searched_for indicates temporal proximity to a donated YouTube search;
subscribed indicates that the watched video’s metadata channel ID appears in the participant’s donated subscription list;
Shorts/Longs labels are derived from metadata;
day/night labels are derived from timestamps interpreted in the configured timezone;
watch-time estimates are derived from metadata duration and the next observed watch event for the same participant;
problematic-view labels are derived from later metadata errors and manual classification.

These indicators should be interpreted as approximations. They are useful for aggregate analysis, but not definitive evidence about individual intention, recommendation logic, or platform causality.

Public Reproduction Notes
The public repository is structured so that readers can follow the method without handling sensitive data.
The public workflow uses:

mock donor files in data/mock_takeout;
a scrambled metadata fixture in data/demo_metadata/meta_data_public.csv;
a manual classification table for generalized availability messages;
rendered notebooks in scripts/;
generated tables and figures in outputs/.

Notebook 03 is the exception: it documents the live metadata collection procedure but is not meant to be executed on the public fake IDs. The rest of the workflow can be followed using the provided public data.

What This Repository Demonstrates
The repository shows how to:

clean donated YouTube watch, search, and subscription files;
extract and standardize YouTube video_ids across donated files;
prepare a video-level metadata table;
join donated histories and metadata at the event level;
classify watches as search-associated or subscription-linked where possible;
distinguish Shorts from Longs using metadata-derived rules;
classify viewing as daytime or nighttime according to a configurable time window;
estimate watch time from metadata duration and the next observed watch event;
attach availability and problematic-view indicators based on metadata/error signals;

The goal is not to reconstruct every individual viewing decision. YouTube does not provide authoritative explanations for why a specific video appeared in a user’s history and we have no access to what a given video meant to a given viewer at time of viewing. However, the workflow produces aggregate exposure indicators that allow insight into how YouTube use differs across users.

What Enrichment Adds
A raw watch-history row tells us that a participant started watching a video at a particular time. After enrichment, the same row can also include:

video duration;
channel and channel identifier;
upload date;
estimated watch time;
view, like, and comment metadata;
language, format, aspect ratio, and other video-level fields;
whether the video is classified as a Short or Long;
whether the watch happened during the configured day or night period;
indication of whether the watch followed an explicit search;
indication of whether the video came from a channel the user is subscribed to;
whether a watched video was later removed by YouTube;
whether the removal was due to potentially problematic content of the video

These data can then be used for user-level assessments like:

share of searched or subscribed watches among all watches
estimated watch time across participants, formats, and routines
distribution of Shorts/Longs across day/night watches
frequency of encountering potentially problematic content

These indicators are imperfect but useful. They are strongest when used to describe patterns across many events or participants, rather than to make definitive claims about single views. The figures below exemplify how the data can be used to quantify YouTube usage and identify differences between individual data donors. These examples are illustrative outputs from the synthetic mock data.






Search and subscription context make it possible to distinguish whether watches followed explicit searches, came from subscribed channels, both, or neither.







The enriched event table allows participant-level comparison across searched, subscribed, Shorts, nighttime, and problematic-view indicators.









Metadata-derived format labels and localized timestamps allow analyses of Shorts/Longs across day and night watching.







Scraped metadata errors and manual classifications make unavailable or potentially problematic watched videos analyzable at the event level.

Handle details And Classify Rows
This step follows the validated selection mechanic from the original workflow: a retained video-watch row must have a standard watch URL, an 11-character video_id, and no details value. Rows with details are excluded from video_histories because in the original data this field captured ads and other non-standard watch-history records.
We still detect Google Ads explicitly and save them separately, so readers can inspect what was excluded.

def details_missing(value):
    # This mirrors the original `details.isna()` selection rule.
    # Lists or dictionaries mean Takeout supplied a details field, so the row is not a clean video watch.
    if isinstance(value, (list, dict)):
        return False
    return bool(pd.isna(value))


def has_google_ads_marker(details):
    if not isinstance(details, list):
        return False
    return any(
        isinstance(item, dict) and item.get("name") == "From Google Ads"
        for item in details
    )


watch_histories["details_missing"] = watch_histories["details"].apply(details_missing)
watch_histories["has_google_ads_marker"] = watch_histories["details"].apply(has_google_ads_marker)
watch_histories["is_youtube_music"] = watch_histories["header"].eq("YouTube Music")

video_mask = (
    watch_histories["has_valid_video_id"]
    & watch_histories["details_missing"]
    & ~watch_histories["is_youtube_music"]
)
music_mask = watch_histories["is_youtube_music"]
ad_watch_mask = watch_histories["has_google_ads_marker"]
other_mask = ~(video_mask | music_mask | ad_watch_mask)


def classify_watch_row(row):
    if row["is_youtube_music"]:
        return "youtube_music"
    if row["has_valid_video_id"] and row["details_missing"]:
        return "video_watch"
    if row["has_google_ads_marker"]:
        return "ad_watch"
    if row["has_valid_video_id"]:
        return "video_with_details"
    return "other_watch_row"


watch_histories["watch_row_type"] = watch_histories.apply(classify_watch_row, axis=1)

